WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.440
A SAST finding is a potential issue reported by the tool that requires review, context, and disposition.

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:15.300
Good findings include the affected file and line, a description of the rule, severity, confidence, an evidence path when available, and remediation guidance.

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:17.760
Severity estimates likely impact.

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:21.340
Confidence estimates how likely the report is to be valid.

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:24.600
Both are useful, but neither replaces human review.

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:28.580
Rule metadata helps reviewers understand what the tool thinks it found.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:35.600
A rule may describe the category, the vulnerable pattern, secure alternatives, references, and examples.

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:38.700
Evidence paths can show how data moves through code.

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:43.000
Duplicate findings may represent one root cause reported in several places.

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:46.660
Noisy rules may report too often or with too little context.

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:52.720
Signal quality is about whether the output leads to risk reduction, not whether the dashboard has a high count.

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:55.580
False positives and false negatives are normal.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:01.080
A false positive is a reported issue that does not apply as described after review.

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:04.820
A false negative is a real issue the tool fails to report.

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:10.400
Teams should tune rules, suppress carefully, document decisions, and revisit exceptions.

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:18.060
Suppression can be useful when a finding is not relevant, but it is risky when it hides real risk without justification, review, or expiration.

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:22.120
A tool finding is not automatically a confirmed vulnerability.

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:24.180
It is a starting point for review.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:34.640
The reviewer asks whether the code is reachable, whether the data is controllable, whether the sink is security-relevant, whether controls are present, and whether the business context increases or reduces impact.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:39.500
Useful SAST programs tune for actionable evidence rather than maximum alert volume.
